---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Rigorous Quality Control (QC) Protocols in GWAS: Sample- and Variant-Level Filtering Pipelines";
const description = "A step-by-step mathematical guide to quality control in genome-wide association studies: call rate thresholds, sex chromosome inbreeding metrics, heterozygosity outlier tests, cryptic relatedness via IBD, and differential missingness.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 733 prose words, 4 display and 24 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "6 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Catalog -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/gwas/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to GWAS Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">GWAS Foundations · Part 3</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Practical & Methodological</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      A genome-wide association study is only as reliable as its quality control pipeline. Technical batch artifacts—such as plate-edge temperature gradients, differential DNA extraction protocols, degraded biobank aliquots, or probe cross-hybridization—can easily generate massive, completely spurious <Katex tex="p < 10^{-50}" /> association signals. This deep dive details the rigorous, two-tier sample- and variant-level filtering protocols required to ensure pristine GWAS data.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#why-qc">1. Why Quality Control is the Foundation of GWAS</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sample-qc">2. Tier 1: Sample-Level Quality Control Protocols</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sex-check">3. Sex Chromosome Inbreeding Discrepancy Checks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#heterozygosity">4. Autosomal Heterozygosity Outlier Detection</a></li>
        <li><a href="#relatedness">5. Cryptic Relatedness & Identity-by-Descent (IBD)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#variant-qc">6. Tier 2: Variant-Level Quality Control Protocols</a></li>
        <li><a href="#diff-missingness">7. Case-Control Differential Missingness & Batch Effects</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">8. Summary & Next Steps in the GWAS Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="why-qc">
      <h2>1. Why Quality Control is the Foundation of GWAS</h2>
      <p>
        In high-throughput genotyping, hundreds of thousands of samples are processed across hundreds of multi-well plates over months or years. If cases and controls are processed on different plates, or if case DNA was extracted from whole blood while control DNA came from saliva, systematic technical differences in missingness and fluorescence intensity will produce hundreds of false-positive "disease associations".
      </p>
      <p>
        Quality control operates in two sequential stages: <strong>Sample-Level QC</strong> (cleaning individuals) followed by <strong>Variant-Level QC</strong> (cleaning genetic markers).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="sample-qc">
      <h2>2. Tier 1: Sample-Level Quality Control Protocols</h2>
      <p>
        Sample QC aims to identify and remove any biological sample whose DNA is degraded, contaminated, mislabeled, or related to other individuals in the cohort.
      </p>

      <h3>Sample Missingness (Call Rate)</h3>
      <p>
        High-quality DNA yields call rates exceeding 99.5%. Any sample with genotype missingness exceeding <strong>2% to 5% (<Katex tex="\text{F\_MISS} > 0.02 - 0.05" />)</strong> must be removed immediately, as high missingness reliably indicates low DNA concentration, fragmentation, or incomplete robotic hybridization.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="sex-check">
      <h2>3. Sex Chromosome Inbreeding Discrepancy Checks</h2>
      <p>
        To detect sample swaps and laboratory pipetting errors, we verify reported clinical sex against X-chromosome genotype heterozygosity.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">X-Chromosome Inbreeding Coefficient</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="F_{\text{inbreeding}} = \frac{O(\text{Hom}) - E(\text{Hom})}{N_{\text{SNP}} - E(\text{Hom})}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="O(\text{Hom})" /> = Observed number of homozygous X-chromosome SNPs (excluding the pseudoautosomal regions PAR1 and PAR2).
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="E(\text{Hom}) = \sum (p_j^2 + q_j^2)" /> = Expected homozygous count under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Expected Values by Sex
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Females (XX):</strong> Possess two X chromosomes. Heterozygosity matches autosomal chromosomes, yielding <Katex tex="F_{\text{inbreeding}} < 0.2" />.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Males (XY):</strong> Possess only one X chromosome (hemizygous). All non-PAR X-chromosome alleles appear homozygous, yielding <Katex tex="F_{\text{inbreeding}} > 0.8" />.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Discrepancies (<Katex tex="0.2 \le F \le 0.8" />):</strong> Flag sample swaps, cross-contamination, or chromosomal aneuploidies (e.g. Klinefelter syndrome 47,XXY or Turner syndrome 45,X). Discrepant samples are excluded.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="heterozygosity">
      <h2>4. Autosomal Heterozygosity Outlier Detection</h2>
      <p>
        Autosomal heterozygosity measures the proportion of heterozygous genotype calls across all autosomes for an individual.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Autosomal Heterozygosity Metric</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="F_{\text{het}} = \frac{N_{\text{total}} - N_{\text{hom}} - \mathbb{E}[N_{\text{het}}]}{N_{\text{total}}}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Individuals with <Katex tex="|F_{\text{het}}| > 3\text{ Standard Deviations}" /> from the cohort mean are excluded:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Excessive Heterozygosity (<Katex tex="F_{\text{het}} \gg +3\text{ SD}" />):</strong> Diagnostic of <strong>sample cross-contamination</strong> (mixing DNA from two individuals during extraction causes homozygous sites to appear heterozygous).
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Deficient Heterozygosity (<Katex tex="F_{\text{het}} \ll -3\text{ SD}" />):</strong> Diagnostic of <strong>inbreeding / consanguinity</strong> or ancestral admixture from a population with lower genetic diversity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="relatedness">
      <h2>5. Cryptic Relatedness & Identity-by-Descent (IBD)</h2>
      <p>
        Standard Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) and Logistic Regression assume that all observations in the sample are statistically independent. If close relatives (e.g. parent-offspring, full siblings, or first cousins) are present in the cohort, the residual errors become correlated, artificially inflating test statistics and false-positive rates.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Pairwise Identity-by-Descent (IBD) & Kinship Coefficient</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="Z_0 = P(\text{IBD} = 0), \quad Z_1 = P(\text{IBD} = 1), \quad Z_2 = P(\text{IBD} = 2)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} = Z_2 + \frac{1}{2} Z_1 \quad \text{(Kinship / Proportion of Shared Genome)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} = 1.0" /> &rarr; Duplicate samples / Monozygotic twins.
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} = 0.50" /> (<Katex tex="Z_0 \approx 0, Z_1 \approx 1, Z_2 \approx 0" />) &rarr; Parent-Offspring pairs.
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} = 0.50" /> (<Katex tex="Z_0 \approx 0.25, Z_1 \approx 0.50, Z_2 \approx 0.25" />) &rarr; Full Siblings.
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} = 0.25" /> &rarr; Second-degree relatives (half-siblings, avuncular, grandparent-grandchild).
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        In an unmixed GWAS cohort, pairs of individuals with <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} > 0.20" /> are identified, and the sample with the lower call rate or the control in a case-control discordant pair is removed to guarantee independence (or alternatively, analyzed using a Linear Mixed Model).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="variant-qc">
      <h2>6. Tier 2: Variant-Level Quality Control Protocols</h2>
      <p>
        Once problematic individuals have been pruned, the remaining genotype matrix undergoes variant-level filtering:
      </p>

      <div class="summary-table-wrap">
        <table class="summary-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Variant QC Filter</th>
              <th>Standard Threshold</th>
              <th>Underlying Artifact Flagged</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Variant Call Rate</strong></td>
              <td>Missingness <Katex tex="< 2\%" /> (<Katex tex="\text{GENO} < 0.02" />)</td>
              <td>Poor oligonucleotide probe synthesis, sequence variation in flanking probe binding site.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Minor Allele Frequency (MAF)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\text{MAF} \ge 0.01" /> (1%)</td>
              <td>Statistical instability in standard error estimation; insufficient power in standard OLS.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_{\text{HWE}} \ge 10^{-6}" /> in controls</td>
              <td>Probe cross-hybridization, cluster boundary overlap, copy number polymorphism.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Differential Missingness</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="p_{\text{diff}} \ge 10^{-5}" /></td>
              <td>Systematic call rate discrepancies between cases and controls.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="diff-missingness">
      <h2>7. Case-Control Differential Missingness & Batch Effects</h2>
      <p>
        One of the most insidious artifacts occurs when a variant exhibits a slightly higher missing rate in cases than in controls.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> The Differential Missingness Trap
        </div>
        <p>
          If case DNA is slightly lower in concentration than control DNA, heterozygous calls for a tricky variant might fail to cross the intensity threshold in cases and be called as missing. In regression, this missingness will distort the allele frequency estimate in cases, creating a <strong>false-positive association signal with <Katex tex="p < 10^{-15}" /></strong>!
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        We perform a Fisher's Exact Test comparing genotype missingness between cases and controls for every variant. Any variant with <Katex tex="p_{\text{diff}} < 10^{-5}" /> is discarded.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 8 -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>8. Summary & Next Steps in the GWAS Series</h2>
      <p>
        Quality control is the unglamorous but essential backbone of human genetics. Passing these rigorous sample- and variant-level gates guarantees that downstream association signals reflect genuine biology rather than laboratory artifacts.
      </p>

      <div class="algo-promo-card">
        <h3>Continue to Part 4: The Mathematical Association Engine</h3>
        <p>
          Derive the exact OLS regression formulas, Frisch-Waugh-Lovell projection, standard error scaling, Wald tests, and statistical power equations.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-association-statistics/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Part 4: Association Statistics</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
